Gem's first point: yes
The transition out of submission needs epistemic evidence, not “I pressed Enter, therefore it happened.”
And 2.2 demonstrably violates that principle because _submit_verified() currently accepts:
composer cleared OR busy OR new output
So busy=True alone can produce SUBMIT CONFIRMED.
We already know from the archived run that this isn't theoretical: busy=True appeared on successful Claude/GPT submissions too. So Gem's “ghost transition” description is damn good.
But I disagree with Gem on one thing
I don't want to require BOTH:
composer clears AND generation indicator appears
That could be unnecessarily strict.
Different AI sites can legitimately expose different pieces of evidence. A UI might clear the composer before the generation control changes, or provide a new response container without giving us a reliable button-state transition.
So I'd phrase the rule more fundamentally:
SUBMITTING cannot become WAITING merely because an action was attempted. It must observe a post-submit state transition that could not reasonably have existed before that submission.
That's the important bit.
And that brings us directly back to the baseline.
Where Gem and I converge
There are really three questions, not one:
1. Did QS cause a submission?
2. Did the target begin processing that submission?
3. Is the thing we're watching actually the response to that submission?
2.2 is currently letting those questions blur together.
That's why the DeepSeek anomaly is so interesting.
The archived log shows QS capturing a baseline, attempting submission, declaring confirmation, and eventually treating output as the response—even though Cozmo had explicitly said he had not actually pressed Enter in that interaction.
GitHub
That's exactly the kind of event that should make us suspicious of causal attribution, not merely timeout length.
