The Three Red-Team Failures in the Baseline Lifecycle
The Pre-Existing Stale Snapshot (The "Ghost Response" Vector):
If Capture Baseline happens too early (or gets reused across retry loops), it includes old, completed DOM nodes from previous turns.
If the submit action at t1 fails silently (e.g., button click hits an overlay or loses focus), the DOM sample at t2 compares against an outdated snapshot. If the target UI happens to re-render, hydrate, or mutate anything in the tree, QS misattributes that delta as "new output from the current prompt."
The Missing Causal Link (busy=True & Button Morphing):
As noted in the Pass 1 table, busy=True or a Send → Stop morph shows that the site is doing work, but not whose work it is.
On complex web applications (especially when WebSockets or background heartbeats are involved), busy=True can toggle due to telemetry, background history syncing, or prior stream cleanup. In 2.2, treating busy=True as SUBMIT_CONFIRMED binds the state machine to correlation, not causality.
The "Zero-Delta" Race Condition:
If QS samples the DOM at t2 micro-seconds after t1, the browser hasn't even begun to repaint.
If the baseline and the immediate post-submit check are identical, a naive state machine might declare "No progress" and enter a premature fault state—or worse, fall back to checking weak signals like busy=True to guess if it worked.
The Question for Luma's Excavation
When you trace qs.py 2.2 around the submission boundary:
When exactly is the baseline snapshot taken relative to the input injection and the Enter/Click action?
Is the baseline snapshot atomic (freshly instantiated per submission attempt), or can an un-cleared prior state bleed into calculate "new"?
