Captain, you just asked the fundamental question of the whole operation: Why do forensic archaeology on a dead build if we're building a new one anyway?
Here is why Luma is holding our feet to the fire on this corpse:
If we don't understand the exact mechanism of the ghost transition in 2.2, we run the risk of re-implementing the exact same vulnerability under a fancier name in 3.0.
If 3.0 has a shiny "Egg Cagey" architecture, but its underlying sensor still uses a permissive OR gate or relies on raw snapshot comparisons, the same ghost will just inhabit the new shell. Dissecting the corpse lets us find the exact broken wire (input_clear OR busy OR new_output) so we know what not to carry over.
Answering Luma's Core Question
Does 2.2 already have the raw data to see the "User-Prompt Thread Transition"?
Answer: No.
Looking directly at how 2.2 scrapes output in qs.py:
_capture_output_snapshot() only queries the output_selector (the response area). It completely ignores the input/user-message containers in the chat thread.
It strips out all structure, returning flat text blobs. It has no concept of "this blob is a User Turn" versus "this blob is an Assistant Turn."
_submit_verified() only looks at whether the composer text string is gone, whether busy is set, or whether the response string array grew.
So 2.2 cannot currently distinguish between:
A new prompt appearing in the chat thread (true submission).
An old prompt re-rendering on scroll (ghost pulse).
The Takeaway for QS 3.0
This gives us a crystal-clear requirement for the Egg Cagey in 3.0:
To verify submission causally, Egg Cagey cannot merely watch the output_selector. It needs a Dual-Anchor Sensor:
Input Anchor: Verify the prompt left the composer.
Thread Anchor: Verify the prompt landed as a new entry in the chat thread.
Once BOTH occur, the Egg Cagey fires the First Causal Pulse, and the state machine safely enters WAITING_FOR_GENERATION.
We've found the broken wire in 2.2. The corpse has yielded its secret!
